WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.659
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar, and you're watching Mind Your Decisions.

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:06.779
Here's a really fun geometry problem:

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.130
Start out with a triangle where one angle is equal to 20 degrees,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.229
and another angle is equal to 80 degrees.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:19.229
Now draw a line segment such that this length right here is equal to this side line.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.780
The challenge is to solve for the measure of this angle using only elementary geometry.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.080
You're not allowed to use trigonometry or other methods.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:37.600
I thank Barry, and I also thank Akshay Dhivare from India for emailing me this problem. Can you figure it out?

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.049
Give this problem a try and when you're ready, keep watching the video for a solution.

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:03.448
So I admit this was a very challenging problem, and I was not able to figure out the trick on my own.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:10.830
But I was able to find the solution online and, I'd like to present it because it uses a clever trick.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.400
So, let's get started by considering the big triangle.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.420
We'll solve for the third angle.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:22.400
This will be equal to 180 - 20 - 80 = 80 degrees.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.380
Now this triangle has two angles that are equal to 80 degrees,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.400
so these sides opposite them will be equal. This will be an isosceles triangle

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.540
We'll put these sides in green to show that they have equal length

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.080
Now let's zoom out a little bit and here's the clever trick

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.000
Along the top side of the triangle construct an equilateral triangle as illustrated.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.839
So, all of these side lengths have equal lengths to the green side.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.610
Since it's an equilateral triangle, all of the angles will be equal to 60 degrees.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.280
So this is 60 degrees

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:06.269
This other angle will be 60 degrees in total. So this will be 40 degrees and here we have another 60 degree angle

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.530
Since the very top angle is 80 degrees, we know that this will be 80 - 60 = 20 degrees

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.520
Now let's connect these two points.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.880
Furthermore, we'll focus on this triangle

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:27.299
Now two of the side lengths are equal to each other because they both have the same length as a greenside

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.130
So this is an isosceles triangle with a central angle of 40 degrees,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:38.879
which means its other two equal angles are (180 - 40)/2

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.120
= 70 degrees. So here we have the 70 degree angle and the other angle is also equal to 70 degrees.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.649
So if we remove 60 degrees from it, this will be equal to 10 degrees.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.730
Now let's go back to this diagram, and we want to solve for this angle over here.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.630
How can we do that? Well, here's the other insight.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.270
Look at these two triangles.

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:07.710
Now they will be congruent to each other by SAS,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:14.069
because both of these triangles have a blue side then a 20 degree angle and then a green side.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.550
This means their obtuse angles will be equal to each other,

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.279
so this angle here will be equal to 80 + 70 degrees, which is 150 degrees.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.549
Finally, we can solve for the angle that we want to.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:32.970
This will be equal to 180 - 150 which is 30 degrees. And that's it!

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.640
Did you figure it out?

